{"body":"## Summary\n\nDuring NF re-entry on Debian Trixie, the installed `/opt/noemaforge` payload was found to be owned by the invoking desktop user (`cat:cat`) and broadly group-writable. This is a packaging/install-process issue, not a host/GPU/runtime issue.\n\nThe local repair was:\n\n```bash\nsudo chown -R root:root /opt/noemaforge\nsudo find /opt/noemaforge -type d -exec chmod 0755 {} \\;\nsudo find /opt/noemaforge -type f -exec chmod 0644 {} \\;\nsudo chmod 0755 \\\n  /opt/noemaforge/bin/* \\\n  /opt/noemaforge/tools/ops/*.sh \\\n  /opt/noemaforge/tools/prep/*.sh \\\n  2>/dev/null || true\n```\n\nAfter this repair, `noemaforge trixie-preflight --json` still passed, confirming the repair did not break the installed runtime surface.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe installed payload scan showed files and directories like:\n\n```text\ndrwxrwxr-x cat:cat /opt/noemaforge\n-rw-rw-r-- cat:cat /opt/noemaforge/lib/noemaforge-common.sh\n-rw-rw-r-- cat:cat /opt/noemaforge/configs/tool-registry.yaml\n-rw-rw-r-- cat:cat /opt/noemaforge/configs/tool-policy.yaml\n-rw-rw-r-- cat:cat /opt/noemaforge/configs/*.json|*.yaml\n```\n\n`toolproxy diag` also reported installed config ownership as `cat:cat`:\n\n```text\ntool_registry: -rw-rw-r-- 664 cat:cat regular file /opt/noemaforge/configs/tool-registry.yaml\ntool_policy: -rw-rw-r-- 664 cat:cat regular file /opt/noemaforge/configs/tool-policy.yaml\n```\n\n## Why this matters\n\n`/opt/noemaforge` is an installed runtime payload used by systemd services. It should not remain writable by the interactive user/group after install. Otherwise, installed scripts/configs can be modified outside the expected release/update path, which weakens runtime integrity and makes diagnostics harder.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nThe installer should install `/opt/noemaforge` with explicit ownership and modes:\n\n```text\n/opt/noemaforge directories: root:root 0755\nregular files:              root:root 0644\nruntime executables/scripts: root:root 0755\nmutable state:              outside /opt, e.g. /var/lib/noemaforge owned by noemaforge:noemaforge as needed\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Add a post-copy ownership/mode normalization step to the installer.\n2. Keep mutable state under `/var/lib/noemaforge`, `/run/noemaforge`, or another documented state path, not writable `/opt`.\n3. Add a post-install verification command that fails if any `/opt/noemaforge` file is owned by the invoking user or group-writable unexpectedly.\n4. Add CI/release-artifact checks for the installed layout, not just the source tree.\n\n## Related\n\nRelated but separate from #212, which covers executable bits for smoke/preflight scripts. This issue covers ownership and writable installed payload integrity.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":213,"state":"OPEN","title":"Installer installs /opt/noemaforge as invoking user and group-writable instead of root-owned runtime payload","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/213"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-214.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\nAfter fixing installed payload modes, `noemaforge smoke` started executing correctly, but it failed in `runtime_only` because the main backend socket was intentionally absent.\n\nThis is a post-install validation semantics issue. `runtime_only` explicitly starts gateway + ToolProxy only and skips the main LLM backend. Therefore a missing `/run/noemaforge/llm/backends/main.sock` should be treated as an expected skip/degraded state for that profile, not as an install/runtime failure.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- Start mode: `sudo noemaforge safe-start --wait --llm-profile=runtime_only`\n- Observed services: gateway + ToolProxy active, no main backend\n\n## Evidence\n\n`safe-start runtime_only` logs say the main backend is intentionally skipped:\n\n```text\n[noemaforge-safe-start] runtime_only profile: enforcing no active main backend.\n[noemaforge-safe-start] stopping stale main backend if present...\n[noemaforge-safe-start] runtime_only profile: skipping modelstore/backend start.\n[noemaforge-safe-start] runtime_only profile: gateway/toolproxy started; main backend not started.\n```\n\nBut `noemaforge smoke` reports:\n\n```text\n[noemaforge-smoke][WARN] backend socket missing: /run/noemaforge/llm/backends/main.sock\n=== NoemaForge smoke ===\nbackend socket: /run/noemaforge/llm/backends/main.sock\nbackend health: fail\nbackend chat:   fail response=<empty>\ngateway socket: /run/noemaforge/llm/gateway.sock\ngateway health: ok\ngateway chat:   non_ok_response response=<empty>\noverall:        fail\n\n=== NoemaForge smoke debug ===\nbackend health error: backend socket missing: /run/noemaforge/llm/backends/main.sock\nbackend chat error: backend socket missing: /run/noemaforge/llm/backends/main.sock\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nFor `runtime_only`, post-install smoke should validate what the profile promises:\n\n```text\ngateway health: ok\nToolProxy health: ok\nmain backend: skipped_expected_runtime_only\noverall: pass or degraded, not fail\n```\n\nThe existing backend-required smoke is still useful, but it should be explicitly tied to `bootstrap_cpu_llm`, `heavy_manual`, or `smoke-main` modes.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Add `noemaforge smoke --profile runtime_only` or `noemaforge smoke --gateway-only`.\n2. Make `noemaforge smoke` infer the active/specified profile when possible.\n3. In `runtime_only`, treat missing main backend socket as expected.\n4. Keep backend health/chat checks mandatory only for profiles where a backend is expected.\n5. Update first-run/re-entry docs so post-install validation uses the correct smoke command for the selected profile.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe current smoke output makes a successful safe-core install/start look failed. It can misdirect debugging toward backend/LLM problems even when the selected install/start profile intentionally avoids launching a model.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":214,"state":"OPEN","title":"Post-install smoke should be profile-aware: runtime_only should not fail on intentionally missing main backend","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/214"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-215.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\nDuring post-install/re-entry testing, `sudo noemaforge safe-start --wait --llm-profile=runtime_only` started the safe core services successfully, but also left gateway/toolproxy systemd units enabled. This is surprising when the machine is explicitly in manual boot mode.\n\nThis is an install/startup policy issue: a one-shot safe-start used for validation should not silently change persistent autostart state unless the operator requested persistence.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- Boot mode before re-entry: manual\n- Command used: `sudo noemaforge safe-start --wait --llm-profile=runtime_only`\n\n## Evidence\n\nBefore re-entry, no NF units were active and unit files were disabled:\n\n```text\nUNIT FILE                               STATE    PRESET\nnoemaforge-autostart-wogui.service      disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-llama@.service               disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-llm-gateway.service          disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-memsentinel.service          disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-toolproxy.service            disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-autostart-gui.timer          disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-llm-backends-manager.timer   disabled enabled\nnoemaforge-modelscan.timer              disabled enabled\n```\n\n`boot-mode show` reported manual mode:\n\n```text\nNoemaForge boot mode: manual\n\nSystemd autostart:\n  gui timer:    disabled\n  gui service:  static\n  wogui:        disabled\n\nAutostart profiles:\n  gui:   runtime_only\n  wogui: bootstrap_cpu_llm\n```\n\nAfter `safe-start runtime_only`, service status showed enabled units:\n\n```text\nnoemaforge-llm-gateway.service - loaded; enabled; active running\nnoemaforge-toolproxy.service   - loaded; enabled; active running\n```\n\nThe safe-start log also says:\n\n```text\n[noemaforge-safe-start] enabling safe core services...\n[noemaforge-safe-start] starting gateway/toolproxy...\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nFor a one-shot post-install validation command:\n\n```text\nsafe-start --llm-profile=runtime_only\n```\n\nshould start the services for the current session, but should not persistently enable them unless the operator also requests an explicit enable/autostart action.\n\nExpected distinction:\n\n```text\nnoemaforge safe-start ...             # start now, no persistence change\nnoemaforge manager enable             # persistently enable manager/modelscan policy\nnoemaforge dashboard autostart-enable # persistently enable dashboard autostart\nnoemaforge boot-mode set ...          # change boot/autostart policy\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Split `start now` from `enable on boot` in `safe-start`.\n2. Add `--enable` or `--persist` if persistent enablement is desired.\n3. In manual boot mode, default to non-persistent start.\n4. Update docs/help text so post-install validation does not accidentally alter boot persistence.\n5. Add a regression test: manual boot mode + `safe-start runtime_only` must not change unit-file enabled/disabled state.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nManual boot mode is used during recovery and controlled NF re-entry. If `safe-start` mutates persistent systemd state, a diagnostic action can unexpectedly change what starts after reboot, making UAT and recovery harder to reason about.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":215,"state":"OPEN","title":"safe-start runtime_only mutates persistent systemd enablement despite manual boot mode","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/215"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-216.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\nDuring post-install/re-entry inspection on `release/0.33.0-dev`, installed systemd units still carried `Version: 0.32.1` headers and depended on multiple `*-hotfix-*` drop-ins.\n\nThis is an install/release packaging hygiene issue. For a prod-ready 0.33.0 installation, required runtime fixes should be integrated into the canonical unit files or explicitly installed as supported override units with clear versioning. The installed unit surface should not look like a stack of temporary hotfixes from earlier recovery work.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- Installed path: `/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-*.service`\n- Runtime profile used during re-entry: `runtime_only`\n\n## Evidence\n\n`systemctl cat noemaforge-llm-gateway.service` showed the canonical unit header still says:\n\n```text\n# File: systemd/noemaforge-llm-gateway.service\n# Zone: release/package\n# Version: 0.32.1\n# Created: 2026-05-14\n# Modified: 2026-05-14\n```\n\nThe same installed gateway unit also relies on drop-ins:\n\n```text\n/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-llm-gateway.service.d/90-hotfix-no-state-directory.conf\n/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-llm-gateway.service.d/95-socket-path-hotfix.conf\n```\n\n`noemaforge-toolproxy.service` had several override drop-ins:\n\n```text\n/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-toolproxy.service.d/05-sel-perms.conf\n/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-toolproxy.service.d/10-root-preflight.conf\n/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-toolproxy.service.d/90-hotfix-no-state-directory.conf\n/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge-toolproxy.service.d/95-socket-path-hotfix.conf\n```\n\nRuntime was working with these drop-ins, but that is exactly the concern: the installed package appears to require accumulated local hotfixes rather than a clean 0.33.0 service definition.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nFor 0.33.0 release/install:\n\n```text\n- systemd unit headers should match the release version or avoid stale version metadata.\n- Required fixes should be integrated into canonical unit files.\n- Supported override files, if intentionally used, should have stable names and documentation, not “hotfix” names.\n- setup/install should be able to recreate a working systemd surface from a clean machine without relying on old local drop-ins.\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Review all `/etc/systemd/system/noemaforge*.service*` outputs from clean install.\n2. Move required `RuntimeDirectory`, `StateDirectory`, socket-path, SEL permission, and preflight fixes into canonical unit files or documented supported drop-ins.\n3. Update unit file headers/version metadata for 0.33.0.\n4. Add an install verification check that reports unexpected `*-hotfix-*` drop-ins after clean install.\n5. Add a clean-install CI/release test that runs `systemctl cat` and verifies the expected unit/drop-in layout.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nA prod-ready installer should produce a reproducible, explainable systemd surface. If the working system depends on stale hotfix overrides, future installs, upgrades, and debugging sessions can diverge from the tested environment.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":216,"state":"OPEN","title":"0.33.0 installed systemd units still rely on hotfix drop-ins and stale 0.32.1 unit metadata","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/216"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-217.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\nAfter fixing installed script modes and `/opt/noemaforge` ownership, `noemaforge mvp-smoke --json` executed and reached real application checks. It reported `36 passed / 3 failed`, but the top-level JSON only exposed generic messages for the failed checks and did not provide actionable stderr paths, command lines, or artifact links in the main output.\n\nThis is a post-install validation UX issue: the installer/re-entry flow needs enough failure detail to move directly from a failed smoke to the exact broken command/config/schema.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- Command: `noemaforge mvp-smoke --json`\n- State dir example: `/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.2qMcC9`\n\n## Evidence\n\nAfter local install-mode repair, `trixie-preflight` passed:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": true,\n  \"passed\": 9,\n  \"warned\": 0,\n  \"failed\": 0\n}\n```\n\nThen `mvp-smoke` ran but returned:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"passed\": 36,\n  \"failed\": 3,\n  \"state\": \"/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.2qMcC9\"\n}\n```\n\nThe failed checks were only summarized as:\n\n```text\npipeline_validate: pipeline validate failed\npipeline_lifecycle: pipeline lifecycle failed\nselftest_core: testbench quick suite failed\n```\n\nThe JSON does include a state directory, but the main failure objects do not directly tell the operator:\n\n```text\n- exact command executed\n- exit code\n- stderr/stdout file path\n- related JSON report path\n- next diagnostic command\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nEach failed post-install smoke check should include an actionable diagnostic payload, for example:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"pipeline_validate\",\n  \"status\": \"fail\",\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"message\": \"pipeline validate failed\",\n  \"command\": \"noemaforge pipeline validate --json\",\n  \"exit_code\": 1,\n  \"stdout_path\": \"/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.x/stdout/pipeline_validate.txt\",\n  \"stderr_path\": \"/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.x/stderr/pipeline_validate.txt\",\n  \"report_path\": \"/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.x/reports/pipeline_validate.json\",\n  \"next\": \"cat .../stderr/pipeline_validate.txt\"\n}\n```\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Capture stdout/stderr for every mvp-smoke subcheck into stable per-check files.\n2. Include command, exit code, stdout/stderr/report paths in each failed check object.\n3. Add `--verbose` / `--explain-failures` mode to print compact failure diagnostics directly.\n4. Ensure the state directory has a predictable structure, e.g. `checks/<id>/stdout.txt`, `checks/<id>/stderr.txt`, `checks/<id>/report.json`.\n5. Add a test that intentionally fails one subcheck and asserts that the JSON contains enough diagnostic metadata.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n`mvp-smoke` is part of the public post-install/re-entry validation path. A failure summary like `pipeline lifecycle failed` is not enough to distinguish a schema regression, state-machine bug, permission problem, missing fixture, or testbench issue. The operator should not have to reverse-engineer the smoke script to find the real stderr.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":217,"state":"OPEN","title":"Post-install mvp-smoke reports 3 failures without actionable stderr/artifact links","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/217"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-218.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\n`noemaforge pipeline validate` fails on the 0.33.0 re-entry machine because several media/admin pipelines reference `media_team`, but the team catalog does not define that team.\n\nThis is not a host, GPU, systemd, or install permission issue. It is a release config/catalog consistency issue.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- Validation state: `/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.2qMcC9`\n- Command surfaced by `mvp-smoke`: `noemaforge pipeline validate`\n\n## Evidence\n\n`/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-pipeline-validate.json` reports:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"pipeline_count\": 101,\n  \"team_count\": 25,\n  \"pattern_count\": 430,\n  \"persona_count\": 23,\n  \"problems\": [\n    \"pipeline image.metadata_and_caption: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline audio.tts_generate: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline audio.music_generate: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline image.photo_generate: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline video.generate: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline video_call.masks_virtual_camera: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline gui.persona_portraits: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline gui.admin_console_api: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline admin.route_music_generation: unknown team media_team\",\n    \"pipeline admin.route_model_evolution: unknown team media_team\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n`/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-pipeline-doctor.json` reports the same as missing team references and confirms runtime itself is OK:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"runtime\": {\n    \"mode\": \"switchable\",\n    \"active_llms\": 0,\n    \"max_active_llms\": 1,\n    \"ok\": true,\n    \"backend_sockets\": []\n  },\n  \"catalogs\": {\n    \"pipelines\": 101,\n    \"teams\": 25,\n    \"patterns\": 430,\n    \"personas\": 23\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe references are present in both installed and source config paths:\n\n```text\n/opt/noemaforge/configs/media-pipeline-catalog.json\n/home/cat/src/NoemaForge/noemaforge/configs/media-pipeline-catalog.json\n/opt/noemaforge/configs/pipelines.json\n/home/cat/src/NoemaForge/noemaforge/configs/pipelines.json\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nRelease catalogs must be self-consistent:\n\n- every pipeline `team` reference must resolve to a defined team;\n- media pipelines should either use a valid existing team, or the release must define `media_team`;\n- `noemaforge pipeline validate` should pass on a clean 0.33.0 install/re-entry state.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Define `media_team` in the team catalog with the roles required by the media/admin pipelines, or remap those pipelines to an existing team if that is the intended design.\n2. Add a release/catalog consistency test that asserts all pipeline team references are resolvable.\n3. Ensure generated/merged installed configs under `/opt/noemaforge/configs` match source configs and validation expectations.\n4. Re-run:\n\n```bash\nnoemaforge pipeline validate\nnoemaforge pipeline doctor --state /tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.2qMcC9 <run_id>\nnoemaforge mvp-smoke --json\n```\n\n## Related\n\nThis is one of the remaining app-level blockers after install/process issues #212-#217 were isolated.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":218,"state":"OPEN","title":"Pipeline catalog validation fails: media pipelines reference missing media_team","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/218"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-219.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\nDuring 0.33.0 re-entry testing, `mvp-smoke` reports a `pipeline_lifecycle` failure because approve/advance mutations are blocked by `degraded_readonly` when the system staffing state is `degraded_selected`.\n\nThis is not a host or systemd failure. It is a mismatch between post-install smoke expectations and the allowed mutation policy under degraded staffing.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- State dir: `/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.2qMcC9`\n- Run ID: `run_20260709T234920Z_public_mwp_mvp_smoke`\n- Staffing state: `degraded_selected`\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe smoke run exists and can be shown from the smoke state dir:\n\n```text\nSMOKE_STATE=/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-smoke.2qMcC9\nRUN_ID=run_20260709T234920Z_public_mwp_mvp_smoke\nstatus=in_progress\ncurrent_stage=status_check\npipeline_id=public_mwp\n```\n\nDirect lifecycle commands against the smoke state return:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"blocked\": true,\n  \"operation\": \"pipeline approve\",\n  \"reason\": \"degraded_readonly\",\n  \"staffing_state\": \"degraded_selected\",\n  \"staffing_summary\": \"/var/lib/noemaforge/bootstrap/firstboot-staffing-summary.json\",\n  \"override\": \"rerun with --allow-degraded or set NOEMAFORGE_ALLOW_DEGRADED_MUTATION=1 after explicit admin approval\"\n}\n```\n\nand:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"blocked\": true,\n  \"operation\": \"pipeline advance\",\n  \"reason\": \"degraded_readonly\",\n  \"staffing_state\": \"degraded_selected\",\n  \"staffing_summary\": \"/var/lib/noemaforge/bootstrap/firstboot-staffing-summary.json\",\n  \"override\": \"rerun with --allow-degraded or set NOEMAFORGE_ALLOW_DEGRADED_MUTATION=1 after explicit admin approval\"\n}\n```\n\nThe run itself is a controlled smoke run in a temporary state dir, not a production pipeline mutation.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nPost-install `mvp-smoke` should be compatible with a degraded-but-safe bootstrap state.\n\nAcceptable designs:\n\n1. If lifecycle mutation is required for smoke, use an explicit smoke-scoped override such as `--allow-degraded` only inside the temporary smoke state.\n2. If degraded mutation must remain blocked, report lifecycle as `skipped_expected_degraded_readonly` rather than `fail`.\n3. If the goal is to test the guardrail itself, assert that `degraded_readonly` blocks production mutation and mark that check as pass.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Decide whether `mvp-smoke` is validating lifecycle mutation or degraded guardrail behavior.\n2. Update `mvp-smoke` accordingly:\n   - either pass `--allow-degraded` for smoke-local state only;\n   - or treat `degraded_readonly` as expected skip/pass under `degraded_selected`;\n   - or split into `pipeline_lifecycle_mutation` and `pipeline_degraded_guardrail` checks.\n3. Include the staffing state and chosen interpretation in `mvp-smoke --json` output.\n4. Add a regression test covering `staffing_state=degraded_selected`.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nA clean post-install/re-entry machine can legitimately be in `degraded_selected` while still being safe and usable. In that state, `mvp-smoke` should not produce a misleading red result unless something actually violates the selected degraded-mode contract.\n\n## Related\n\nRelated to #217 for diagnostic output quality and #218 for the separate catalog validation failure.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":219,"state":"OPEN","title":"mvp-smoke pipeline lifecycle check is blocked by degraded_readonly in degraded_selected staffing state","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/219"}

===== .codex/tasks/0.33.0-prod-ready/issues/issue-220.json =====
{"body":"## Summary\n\nDuring 0.33.0 re-entry diagnostics, `noemaforge pipeline validate` produced JSON artifacts through `mvp-smoke`, but the direct CLI command rejected `--json`:\n\n```text\nnoemaforge pipeline: error: unrecognized arguments: --json\n```\n\nThis is an operator UX/API consistency issue. Many adjacent commands expose `--json`, and post-install diagnostics naturally expect validation commands to support it.\n\n## Environment\n\n- NoemaForge CLI: `0.33.0`\n- Branch: `release/0.33.0-dev`\n- Command attempted: `noemaforge pipeline validate --json`\n- Related artifact: `/tmp/noemaforge-mvp-pipeline-validate.json`\n\n## Evidence\n\nDirect command:\n\n```bash\nnoemaforge pipeline validate --json\n```\n\nreturned:\n\n```text\nusage: noemaforge pipeline [-h] [--root ROOT] [--state STATE]\n                           {catalog,run,list,show,toolproxy-policy,next,snapshot,advance,approve,pause,resume,fail,cancel,artifact,worktree,summary,next-packet,export,dashboard-state,patterns,validate,doctor,readiness,repair,gate,template-import,template-append,context-lint,stage-validate,stage-smoke,compact,queue,metrics,schema-validate,event-log,lease,executor-step,selection-refresh,member,policy} ...\nnoemaforge pipeline: error: unrecognized arguments: --json\n```\n\nAt the same time, `mvp-smoke` writes JSON output for this validation surface:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"pipeline_count\": 101,\n  \"team_count\": 25,\n  \"pattern_count\": 430,\n  \"persona_count\": 23,\n  \"problems\": [\n    \"pipeline image.metadata_and_caption: unknown team media_team\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nOne of these should be true and documented:\n\n1. `noemaforge pipeline validate --json` is supported and prints machine-readable JSON; or\n2. `noemaforge pipeline validate` always emits JSON and `--json` is accepted as a no-op compatibility flag; or\n3. the command has a clearly documented alternative such as `--format json`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n1. Add `--json` support to `pipeline validate`, ideally as a no-op if JSON is already the default.\n2. Apply the same consistency review to `pipeline doctor`, `pipeline readiness`, and other post-install validation commands.\n3. Update examples/docs so operators can copy/paste reliable diagnostic commands.\n4. Add a CLI surface regression test that all post-install validation commands either accept `--json` or document a canonical JSON flag.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nDuring install/re-entry triage, command consistency reduces false starts. The current mismatch makes a valid diagnostic instinct fail before reaching the actual catalog problem.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOSdiUqc8AAAACjNlHzQ","name":"bug","description":"Something isn't working","color":"d73a4a"}],"number":220,"state":"OPEN","title":"CLI consistency: pipeline validate emits validation JSON but rejects --json","url":"https://github.com/Sinev-Maksim/NoemaForge/issues/220"}

