# NoemaForge project context

NoemaForge is being prepared as a local-first `0.32.1` prelaunch package. The active package root is `noemaforge/`; runtime contracts live in `noemaforge/src`, deterministic policies live in `noemaforge/configs`, and executable coverage lives in `noemaforge/tests`.

The current release discipline treats documentation layout as a release gate, not as a cleanup preference. Markdown that remains active must live in subfolders under `noemaforge/docs`, `helpers` or `prelaunch`. The only Markdown files allowed directly in `noemaforge/docs` are `README.md`, `Manifest.md` and `TODO.md`. Changelog and release-history text is canonical in `noemaforge/docs/history/CHANGELOG.md`.

Legacy root and top-level Markdown can contain useful historical context, but it is no longer part of the active package surface. Useful TODO content and standalone wiki pages are migrated into canonical `noemaforge/docs` locations before obsolete source files are moved to project trash. Trash is a quarantine area for normal autonomous work and is excluded from active-tree hygiene scans, release manifests and package checksums.

Legacy live-debug host names, old public-docs path mentions and raw research dumps are not allowed in the active tree. Useful prose is rewritten into neutral release language, while obsolete raw sources and duplicate legacy manifests are moved to project trash for the daily cleanup cycle.

The strict docs gate is implemented by `noemaforge/configs/docs-hygiene-policy.json` and `noemaforge/src/docs_hygiene_runtime.py`. It validates required canonical files, direct docs-root limits, forbidden documentation folders, forbidden active text, active-file readability, canonical changelog references and checked production-AI TODO items. QA and performance tests keep the scanner deterministic on local fixtures without network or hardware dependencies. Active files that appear in directory listings but cannot be stat/read-opened are treated as release-hygiene failures rather than silently skipped.

The 0.32.2 hardening pass extends that gate to reject legacy host-specific names and old public-docs path strings in active UTF-8 files. Release guidance now uses neutral target-host wording so active docs remain reusable across machines while still preserving target-only validation requirements.

YAML inventory readability is now an explicit fallback gate for local release validation. `yaml-inventory-readability-core` scans active `.yaml` and `.yml` files under `noemaforge` and `prelaunch`, requires UTF-8 readability, rejects tab indentation and checks simple flow bracket balance with only the Python standard library. The gate is intentionally scoped as `readability_and_yaml_lite_syntax_only`; it does not replace full YAML semantic parsing where PyYAML is available, but it prevents YAML files from becoming invisible to local prelaunch checks. Closed by `yaml-inventory-readability-core`.

Manifest and checksum exclusion is now executable instead of convention-only. `manifest-checksum-exclusion-core` validates the root package manifest, the NoemaForge docs manifest, manifest SHA sidecars, root SHA256 lists and `noemaforge/checksums/SHA256SUMS` against the active tree while excluding project trash and generated cache directories. The gate catches stale file counts, excluded-path leaks, missing checksum entries and hash mismatches before release evidence is trusted. Closed by `manifest-checksum-exclusion-core`.

Release-history naming is now guarded separately from Markdown placement. `release-artifact-name-guard-core` keeps `history/CHANGELOG.md` as the canonical release-history target and scans the active tree for parallel release-note, extra changelog, verification-report and raw research/source report filenames. The scanner skips project trash and generated caches, so quarantined legacy material can remain outside active release evidence while new parallel release artifacts fail locally. Closed by `release-artifact-name-guard-core`.

Setup default path is now a canonical onboarding contract rather than scattered helper advice. `setup-default-path-core` points new operators to release unpack or git clone, then root `./setup.sh` in VM mode first, with host install only after explicit operator choice; Windows helper scripts remain optional side tools and are never required for the canonical path. Setup default path boundary: The blessed onboarding path is release unpack or git clone, then root ./setup.sh in VM mode first, then host install only by explicit operator choice; Windows helpers are optional side tools and are never required for the canonical path.

Setup front door is the single command surface for bootstrap and firstboot. `setup-front-door-core` keeps root `setup.sh` as the one setup entry point for vm, host and docker-dev modes, verifies install-root, data-root, model-profile, with-share and offline-after-setup flags, and requires bootstrap and firstboot progress phases to be emitted from that path. Setup front door boundary: Root setup.sh is the single setup front door, supports vm/host/docker-dev plus install-root/data-root/model-profile/with-share/offline-after-setup flags, and emits bootstrap/firstboot progress phases so newcomers do not discover helper scripts manually.

Firstboot progress is an operator-facing view over the same JSON status and event stream. `firstboot-progress-view-core` renders the canonical seed_copy, host_preflight, vault_scan, inbox_normalize, candidate_staging, role_staffing, epoch_apply and reboot_pending phases, and ends with next actions so the operator can continue without reading raw event files. Firstboot progress boundary: Firstboot progress is a human-readable CLI/TUI surface over JSON status/events, shows seed_copy/host_preflight/vault_scan/inbox_normalize/candidate_staging/role_staffing/epoch_apply/reboot_pending phases, and ends with next actions so the operator always knows the current step.

