Model-health filtering is now an executable firstboot contract. `model-health-candidate-filter-core` validates that `role-candidate-map.filtered.json` does not select any model whose `model-health-registry.json` state is failed or excluded, keeps the check offline with synthetic fixtures and attaches the eval pack to `firstboot-model-selection`. Closed by `model-health-candidate-filter-core`.

Previous install context is now explicitly detached from active runtime. `previous-install-context-core` validates canonical active install/state/share/modelstore/bootstrap roots, rejects legacy share prefixes and backup/migration context in active roots, and permits previous install, backup and migration records only as archive or readonly evidence. Closed by `previous-install-context-core`.

Pytest suite execution is partitioned for this container's release validation path. `pytest-suite-partition-core` treats monolithic pytest as an unsafe local execution shape after the observed pipeline runtime hang, validates bounded shard plans, isolates pipeline-runtime tests and requires per-shard timeouts while preserving targeted unittest coverage. Closed by `pytest-suite-partition-core`.

Roadmap status legends are now explicit prose labels instead of Markdown task-list syntax. `backlog-status-legend-core` keeps the canonical roadmap legend from producing false open TODO entries such as `planned`, and its scanner fails if a status legend reverts to task-list-shaped markers. Closed by `backlog-status-legend-core`.

Admin GUI job progress now has an offline-validated streaming surface. `job-progress-stream-core` adds `/api/jobs/stream` as a deterministic SSE snapshot stream that emits `jobs_snapshot` and `job_progress` events, while the dashboard uses `EventSource` and keeps `/api/jobs` polling as a fallback for browsers or local serving modes without streams. Closed by `job-progress-stream-core`.

Admin GUI smalltalk now exposes its backend choice without weakening control routing. `admin-llm-smalltalk-core` validates the optional local LLM chat path, the deterministic fallback path and the `conversation_backend` API field, while explicit pipeline/control requests still route before conversational handling. Closed by `admin-llm-smalltalk-core`.

Admin GUI runtime observation now has dedicated gateway/backend cards. `runtime-observer-cards-core` adds `/api/runtime/observer-cards`, includes the same `observer_cards` in telemetry, and renders gateway service/socket plus main backend service/socket smoke affirmation in the Runtime card without requiring a live target host during validation. Closed by `runtime-observer-cards-core`.

The Pipeline Dock now includes a draft-only drag/drop editor. `pipeline-dragdrop-editor-core` validates stage reorder, add/remove controls, `drag_drop_pipeline_editor` draft normalization and `/api/pipelines/draft` saves, while keeping edited pipelines inactive until review. Closed by `pipeline-dragdrop-editor-core`.

Installed Admin GUI artifact cards now provide local-first artifact affordances without exposing arbitrary files. `artifact-card-affordance-core` enriches artifact records with guarded Open and Download URLs, adds `/api/artifacts/open` previews plus `/api/artifacts/download` file responses, and restricts both endpoints to NoemaForge state roots. Closed by `artifact-card-affordance-core`.

The Admin GUI now has a dedicated dashboard backend endpoint instead of relying only on the broader GUI state route. `dashboard-api-endpoint-core` adds `/api/dashboard` and `/api/dashboard/state`, marks the payload with `dashboard_backend` metadata and keeps `/api/gui/state` as a compatibility fallback for older dashboard clients. Closed by `dashboard-api-endpoint-core`.

The Admin GUI main chat locale surface is now explicitly validated. `locale-main-chat-surface-core` requires `/api/locales` to return message dictionaries for each installed locale pack and verifies that composer, depth controls, pipeline dock, artifact download and speaker labels are applied from those messages instead of leaving mixed RU/EN fallback labels in the default chat view. Closed by `locale-main-chat-surface-core`.

The public polish choice is now executable rather than aspirational. `public-showcase-guided-scenario-core` selects `polished_admin_gui_guided_scenario`, exposes `/api/public-showcase/scenario` and the Admin GUI Public scenario card, and keeps the path local-first: no live backend demo, no hidden backend start, no auto-apply and no packaging step. The final target-machine replay remains a separate open validation item. Closed by `public-showcase-guided-scenario-core`.

The default model-runtime policy is now an executable decision instead of an open product question. `default-model-runtime-policy-core` selects `cpu_safe_always_on_with_gpu_on_demand`: NoemaForge defaults to a CPU-safe runtime posture, keeps GPU work explicit and staged through the existing device-policy boundary, preserves one active heavy worker and does not enable boot-time LLM autostart. The policy is validated against `runtime-device-policy.json`, model profiles, runtime default safety, device-policy staging and the Admin GUI fallback state.

CPU and GPU model scorecards are now explicit namespaces, not mixed evidence. `cpu-gpu-scorecard-separation-core` validates `runtime_device` metadata in scorecard writers, routes CPU records to `model_scorecards/cpu`, routes GPU and CUDA records to `model_scorecards/gpu`, and keeps unspecified legacy scorecards separate from device-qualified evaluation evidence.

Approved privileged GUI jobs now share one concrete runner boundary instead of loose sudo handoffs. `privileged-gui-job-runner-core` keeps the browser plan-first, but attaches `polkit_approval_required` runner metadata, deterministic approval tokens, the `org.noemaforge.privileged-jobs.run` polkit action and a `--dry-run` default to epoch apply, Continue model selection and Vault re-inventory jobs. Vault re-inventory preserves its visible fallback command while validating the privileged runner as three explicit command steps. Non-dry-run execution remains outside the browser and requires explicit approval evidence.

Production GUI install is now a composed offline readiness contract rather than a single unchecked shell script. `production-gui-installer-core` ties together root `setup.sh`, the promoted installer, package dry-run validation, setup front-door/mode contracts, stateful Admin GUI assets, timer-driven GUI systemd units, boot-mode CLI controls and recovery helpers. Production GUI installer boundary: dry-run and selftest are the first path, live host install is root-gated, GUI autostart is timer-driven, Admin GUI starts without hidden LLM autostart, and live target validation remains a separate hardware-dependent gate.

Clean install validation with the canonical share is now an executable readiness contract, not a closed target result. `clean-install-share-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_clean_install_evidence`, requires `./setup.sh` dry-run/selftest evidence, an operator-approved host install with `/mnt/noemaforge-share`, nofail/automount share evidence, detached previous-install context, post-install Trixie preflight, emergency-mode checks and a forensics archive. The local validator only checks policy, registry, docs and evidence-manifest shape; the clean-install TODO remains open until NoemaForge produces target-machine evidence.

The patched10 full-composite real run also has an executable readiness contract now. `full-composite-target-run-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_full_composite_evidence`, requires patched10 install baseline evidence, target preflight, operator-approved `--full_composite 0` command plan, a run transcript with TTY status, first-start summary artifacts, display/abort recovery evidence and a forensics archive. The validator stays offline and does not run `first-start`, systemd, GUI recovery or forensics commands; the target run TODO remains open until the NoemaForge host produces and archives those artifacts.

The SSH-assisted patched10 full-composite run is also explicit but still target-machine-only. `full-composite-ssh-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_ssh_evidence`, requires SSH service and listener evidence, an operator-approved known-host access plan with no archived secrets, an SSH-observed `--full_composite 0` transcript, SSH abort/display recovery evidence and a redacted forensics archive. The local validator checks only policy, docs, examples and registry attachment; it never opens SSH sessions or runs remote commands.

The `/run/nologin` recovery confirmation is now its own target-evidence contract. `nologin-recovery-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_nologin_recovery_evidence`, requires pre-recovery nologin and display baseline, operator-approved abort cleanup, post-recovery `/run/nologin` absence, display-manager or GDM active state and a forensics archive. Local validation stays offline and cannot run systemd, recovery or forensics commands.

The Debian Trixie emergency GUI recovery validation is also explicit but remains target-machine-only. `emergency-gui-recovery-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_emergency_gui_recovery_evidence`, requires display-manager and GDM baseline evidence, operator-approved `sudo noemaforge pause --wait` and `sudo noemaforge gui-rescue --wait` transcripts, display-manager alias-to-GDM evidence, post-rescue `/run/nologin` absence and a reviewed forensics archive. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and docs shape; it does not call systemd, recovery or archive commands.

The share automount reboot guard is also explicit and target-only. `share-automount-reboot-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_share_reboot_evidence`, requires fstab and automount unit evidence for `/mnt/noemaforge-share`, operator-approved reboot evidence, post-reboot emergency and rescue target state, post-reboot share access and a forensics archive. The local validator only checks policy, registry, docs and evidence shape; it does not reboot, mount shares or query systemd.

The post-reboot validation archive is now explicit as the next target-only evidence bundle. `post-reboot-validation-archive-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_post_reboot_archive_evidence`, requires post-reboot boot ID and failed-unit evidence, gateway/main-backend/display-manager service state, operator-approved live smoke transcripts, forensics and journal archive hashes, and a wiki patch bundle manifest that references the target evidence. The local validator only checks policy, registry, docs and example shape; it does not run systemd, journal, forensics, live smoke or wiki patch commands.

The post-reboot service health check is now split out from the broader archive gate. `post-reboot-service-health-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_post_reboot_service_health_evidence`, requires the target boot ID and system-running state, gateway service health, operator-approved manual `noemaforge-llama@main` start/stop evidence, ToolProxy service health, gateway/ToolProxy socket or API smoke output and a reviewed archive hash. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run systemd, socket probes or LLM commands.

The composite post-reboot GPU/GDM/gateway/ToolProxy line is now an executable target-evidence contract instead of a broad reminder. `post-reboot-gpu-gdm-gateway-toolproxy-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_post_reboot_gpu_gdm_gateway_toolproxy_evidence`, requires boot baseline, operator approval, display-manager/GDM state, NVIDIA signal, gateway and ToolProxy service state, gateway and ToolProxy socket smoke evidence, redaction review and a hashed archive. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run systemd, NVIDIA, gateway, ToolProxy, LLM or archive commands.

The gateway plus main-backend live smoke is now narrowed as its own target-evidence contract. `gateway-main-live-smoke-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_gateway_main_live_smoke_evidence`, requires gateway and `noemaforge-llama@main` service/socket baseline, operator approval, manual main-backend start evidence, gateway socket/status evidence, gateway smoke and chat transcripts, backend stop evidence and a reviewed archive hash. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run systemd, socket probes, gateway commands or LLM commands.

The Debian Trixie preflight run is now an explicit target-evidence contract instead of an implied operator note. `trixie-preflight-target-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_trixie_preflight_evidence`, requires operator approval for the read-only `sudo noemaforge trixie-preflight --json` command, JSON parse evidence, Debian/kernel baseline, dependency surface, runtime socket snapshot and archive hash. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run sudo, preflight, systemd, socket probes or remediation commands.

Post-failure support capture is also explicit. `failure-forensics-bundle-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_failure_forensics_bundle_evidence`, requires a target failure timestamp and surface, operator approval for `sudo noemaforge forensics --dry-run` and `sudo noemaforge forensics`, bundle path, SHA256 and file inventory, redaction evidence for secrets, model weights, Vault content, browser profiles and token paths, runtime log coverage and a reviewed findings/follow-up record. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run sudo, forensics, journal, systemd or upload commands.

The target-hardware GUI recovery path now has a separate evidence contract from the broader emergency GUI recovery slice. `target-gui-recovery-path-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_gui_recovery_path_evidence`, requires pre-recovery display-manager/GDM and graphical target state, operator-approved `sudo noemaforge pause --wait`, operator-approved `sudo noemaforge gui-rescue --wait`, proof that pause preceded rescue, post-rescue display state, `/run/nologin` absence, a forensics archive hash and inspected follow-up notes. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run sudo, recovery, systemd or archive commands.

The systemd/GDM/NVIDIA live-validation line is now narrowed from the broader target-live gate. `systemd-gdm-nvidia-live-validation-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_systemd_gdm_nvidia_live_validation_evidence`, requires boot ID, system-running and failed-unit state, operator approval, display-manager/GDM and graphical target state, NVIDIA driver/device/module evidence, secure-boot and kernel driver log evidence, `/run/nologin` and seat recovery guards, and a reviewed archive hash. The local validator checks only policy, example, registry and documentation shape; it does not run systemd, GDM, NVIDIA, journal, loginctl or archive commands.

The live self-improvement testbench run now has its own target-evidence contract. `live-testbench-suite-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_live_testbench_suite_evidence`, requires target baseline, operator approval, live-suite catalog, the exact `noemaforge testbench run --suite live --include-live --json` transcript, resource telemetry artifacts, baseline comparison, wiki patch manifest and archive hash. The local validator checks policy, example, registry and documentation shape only; it does not run testbench, live suites, GPU probes, subprocesses or wiki patch commands.

The final Admin GUI scenario replay also has a target-evidence contract now. `final-gui-scenario-replay-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_target_final_gui_scenario_replay_evidence`, binds the replay to `polished_admin_gui_guided_scenario`, and requires target baseline, operator approval, Admin GUI start/health, greeting transcript, routed pipeline launch, Dev Team action, model-evolution action, full transcript, artifact hashes, redaction review, archive hash and version-bump guard evidence. The local validator checks policy, example, registry and docs only; it does not start the GUI, browser, pipelines, Dev Team, model-evolution, subprocesses or archive commands.

The local dashboard launcher also owns the user-level dashboard autostart boundary. `dashboard-launcher-core` now validates `autostart-enable`, `autostart-disable` and `autostart-status` as operator-scoped systemd user timer commands under `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, with dry-run output, no root requirement and no hidden LLM or media backend startup.

SmartHome Local Control is an offline local-first control surface, not a cloud automation bridge. `smarthome-local-control-core` binds smart plugs, switches, vacuums, cameras and sensors to a local server by default, exposes MQTT/Home Assistant/Zigbee/Z-Wave/Matter adapter surfaces, requires visible privacy state for cameras and microphones, and keeps automation actions behind emergency pause, human override and SR/SSR review evidence.

Media capture privacy is now an explicit gate for the non-GGUF media path. `media-capture-privacy-core` covers `voice.capture_live`, `voice.transcribe_live` and `camera.mask_virtual_camera`: microphone and camera use is off by default, every live capture or virtual camera mask plan needs operator consent, visible privacy state, a manual command and no autostart, and the default voice backend policy remains disabled until the operator selects local devices.

Media adapter observability is now an executable local contract. `media-adapter-telemetry-core` covers the voice, music, photo, video, image-analysis and camera-mask pipelines: each adapter has a plan-only selftest case and required telemetry for wall time, CPU time, RSS, disk reads/writes, artifact bytes and status, while live backend execution remains optional and explicit.

Final live-media backend selection is now a separate target-gated contract. `media-backend-selection-readiness-core` records `blocked_until_explicit_media_backend_selection`, requires explicit operator selection records for VLM, STT, TTS, music generation, image generation, video generation and segmentation/mask adapters, plus adapter command contracts, privacy consent gates where capture is involved, media telemetry/selftest evidence, target live-smoke transcripts and generated artifact hashes. The local validator only checks policy, registry, docs and example shape; it does not start media backends, capture devices or download weights.

Media wiki patches now preserve media evidence instead of reducing multimodal changes to prose. `media-wiki-patch-core` extends the offline wiki patch runtime with optional `--media-capability-report` and `--artifact-manifest` inputs, copies them into `media_capability_delta.json` and `generated_artifacts.json`, records them in `wiki_patch_manifest.json`, and requires canonical docs plus registry attachment for the contract.

Knowledge purpose artifacts are now a typed pre-ingest contract. `knowledge-purpose-artifacts-core` requires every knowledge realm or project to declare mission, scope boundaries, out-of-scope topics, expected source quality and update policy before ingest, lint or review can accept knowledge into the graph. Knowledge purpose boundary: Every knowledge realm/project has a typed purpose artifact with mission, scope boundaries, out-of-scope topics, expected source quality and update policy; ingest, lint and review must reference it before adding or approving knowledge.

Knowledge graph lint is an offline maintenance contract for the graph layer. `knowledge-graph-lint-core` detects orphan concepts, unsupported claims, stale passages, unresolved conflicts and weak realm bridges, then emits Administrator and Surgeon work items for pre-start and scheduled maintenance loops. Knowledge graph lint boundary: Graph maintenance detects orphan concepts, unsupported claims, stale passages, unresolved conflicts and weak realm bridges, then surfaces explicit Administrator/Surgeon maintenance work for pre-start or scheduled maintenance loops.

Knowledge core relations are frozen as a typed graph contract. `knowledge-core-relations-gates-core` keeps Source, Passage, Claim, Entity, Concept, Conflict, Trail, TaskContext, Decision and Artifact relations explicit, and requires Passage, Claim, Conflict and Concept publication gates to return auto_publish, review or quarantine before retrieval can treat graph objects as published. Knowledge core relations boundary: Canonical knowledge relations are frozen as typed links among Source, Passage, Claim, Entity, Concept, Conflict, Trail, TaskContext, Decision and Artifact; publication gates for Passage, Claim, Conflict and Concept must emit auto_publish, review or quarantine decisions before retrieval can treat objects as published.

Role corpus binding is deterministic and corpus-aware. `role-corpus-binding-core` keeps role-scoped JSONL suites bound to available seed, external-library or state-KG corpora, and reports missing required corpora as N/A with `dataset_missing` instead of claiming synthetic coverage. Role corpus binding boundary: Role evaluation keeps role-scoped JSONL suites, binds roles to real seed/external corpora when available, and marks missing required corpora as N/A instead of silently faking coverage.

Hypergraph error improvement is the reviewed feedback loop for knowledge extraction defects. `hypergraph-error-improvement-core` keeps error_events, corrections and regression_cases in the local error-learning store, separates source defects from labeling/chunking/extraction/linking defects, and only reuses approved corrections for retraining and evaluation. Hypergraph error improvement boundary: Error learning stores error_events, corrections and regression_cases, separates source defects from labeling/chunking/extraction/linking defects, reuses approved corrections for retraining and evaluation, and records the model, run and profile that produced each error.

Graph projection views are derived review surfaces, not canonical graph storage. `graph-projection-views-core` generates wiki_markdown, operator_summary, task_context and conflict_review artifacts from graph state with source graph refs, graph digests, generated_at timestamps, projection types and explicit not-source-of-truth notices, so docs and wiki-like pages stay inspectable without becoming the knowledge authority. Graph projection boundary: Wiki-like pages are projections derived from graph state: every projection carries source graph refs, graph digest, generated_at, projection type, and a not-source-of-truth notice so human-readable pages never become the canonical knowledge store. Closed by `graph-projection-views-core`.

Graph patch provenance is now the review contract between graph state and wiki patch publication. `graph-patch-provenance-core` defines a `GraphPatch` format with source and target graph refs, a source graph digest, trace ID, actor role, review decision, rollback plan and per-operation provenance. The runtime validates the schema and example bundle offline, records that graph patches are not auto-applied, and keeps provenance attached to claims, edges, conflict resolutions and artifact links before those patches can inform human-readable wiki outputs. Closed by `graph-patch-provenance-core`.

Artifact registry rows now have a shared contract before pipeline outputs, review records or graph patches are treated as evidence. `artifact-registry-table-core` defines the required table names, columns, SHA256 evidence, relative artifact paths, trace IDs, producer metadata, review states and graph patch references. It is a JSON-table contract rather than a live storage migration: validation proves record shape and references locally without mutating artifact storage. Closed by `artifact-registry-table-core`.

